 might remove too much material and weaken the metal. If the metal item is very heavy, you can likely sand with a power sander.. Sand the metal and hardened filler with a sanding block and 80-grit sandpaper, if necessary, and wipe off the dust with a dry rag. Sanding with coarse sandpaper is only necessary if you were unable to scrape off the excess filler evenly, which leaves ridges and bumps of filler on the metal.\\\\n\\\\npassage 2:Secure an 80-grit flapper wheel to a grinder. Sand the pitted metal with the flapper wheel until no rust or old paint remains on its surface. Remove the flapper wheel. Attach a wire wheel to the arbor of the grinder. Wire wheel the entire surface of the metal to remove debris from the metal pits.Spray degreasing cleaner on the surface of the metal.and the pitted metal with the flapper wheel until no rust or old paint remains on its surface. Remove the flapper wheel. Attach a wire wheel to the arbor of the grinder.\\\\n\\\\npassage 3:Step by step instructions on how to repair pitted pot metal or zinc die cast parts. Classic car owners know that pits are prevalent on most antique autos, and typically this repair is difficult due to the low melting point of pot metal.tep by step instructions on how to repair pitted pot metal or zinc die cast parts. Classic car owners know that pits are prevalent on most antique autos, and typically this repair is difficult due to the low melting point of pot metal.\\\\n\\\\n\\",\\"question\\":\\"how to fill pitted metal\\"}","answer":"Based on the provided passages, here\'s how to fill pitted metal:\\n\\nPassage 1 suggests using a sanding block and 80-grit sandpaper to sand the pitted area aggressively. This helps remove the excess filler material and smooth out the surface. If the metal item is heavy, a power sander can be used, but it may remove too much material. After sanding, wipe off the dust with a dry rag. If there are still ridges and bumps of filler on the surface, additional sanding with coarser sandpaper (such as 120-grit) may be necessary.\\n\\nPassage 2 provides an alternative method for filling pitted metal. First, secure an 80-grit flapper wheel to a grinder and sand the pitted metal until no rust or old paint remains on the surface. Then, remove the flapper wheel and attach a wire wheel to the grinder\'s arbor. Use the wire wheel to remove any debris from the pitted areas. Finally, spray degreasing cleaner on the surface of the metal and repeat the sanding process until no rust or old paint remains.\\n\\nPassage 3 provides step-by-step instructions for repairing pitted pot metal or zinc die cast parts. The process involves cleaning the pitted area with a wire brush, applying a filler material, and then sanding and painting the repaired area.\\n\\nTherefore, based on the provided passages, the best way to fill pitted metal is to first sand the area aggressively with a sanding block and 80-grit sandpaper, followed by additional sanding with coarser sandpaper if necessary. Alternatively, you can use a grinder with an 80-grit flapper wheel to remove rust and old paint, followed by a wire wheel to remove debris from the pitted areas.","source_segments":[{"index":0,"byte_start":0,"byte_end":122},{"index":1,"byte_start":122,"byte_end":237},{"index":2,"byte_start":237,"byte_end":312},{"index":3,"byte_start":312,"byte_end":443},{"index":4,"byte_start":443,"byte_end":663},{"index":5,"byte_start":663,"byte_end":759},{"index":6,"byte_start":759,"byte_end":785},{"index":7,"byte_start":785,"byte_end":834},{"index":8,"byte_start":834,"byte_end":1062},{"index":9,"byte_start":1062,"byte_end":1088},{"index":10,"byte_start":1088,"byte_end":1234},{"index":11,"byte_start":1234,"byte_end":1469},{"index":12,"byte_start":1469,"byte_end":1665}],"answer_sentence_indices":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7],"answer_offsets":[{"index":0,"byte_start":0,"byte_end":167},{"index":1,"byte_start":167,"byte_end":240},{"index":2,"byte_start":240,"byte_end":333},{"index":3,"byte_start":333,"byte_end":3