ave been there since it opened, and those people are excruciatingly bored or scared or both.\\" To combat opiate use among inmates, prison guards use narcotics-sniffing dogs for inspections, Walker said. \\"It\'s pretty pathetic. I have clients (in Souza prison) who are heroin addicts who are trying to get clean,\\" Walker said. There also are gangs in the prison, she said. As for violence, one elderly inmate was beaten to death last year, Walker said. If Hernandez finds himself in solitary confinement, it won\'t be pleasant, Walker said. \\"Solitary confinement is a kind of torture that no one does well in. The lack of meaningful sensory stimulus and contact drives people mad,\\" Walker said. Initially, \\"prison officials may feel they have to put him there for his safety, but I hope not,\\" Walker said. \\"It\'s very hard time.\\" Opinion: The shame of Aaron Hernandez.\\n","answer":"Aaron Hernandez, the former NFL player convicted of murder, will be sent to Massachusetts\' Souza-Baranowski Correctional Center, a maximum-security prison known for its high-tech security measures and strict conditions. The prison is equipped with 366 cameras and a microwave detection perimeter, and inmates are kept in their cells for 19 hours a day. The cells are small and sparse, with metal fixtures and limited furnishings. The prison has a reputation for being violent, with reports of opiate use and gang activity. Hernandez\'s safety may be a concern, as he may be targeted by other inmates or gangs. The prison\'s superintendent is known for being thorough and decent, and will likely take steps to ensure Hernandez\'s safety. However, the conditions in the prison are harsh, and the lack of meaningful stimulus and contact can drive inmates mad. It remains to be seen where Hernandez will be placed within the prison, but it is likely that he will be kept in solitary confinement for his own protection.","source_segments":[{"index":0,"byte_start":0,"byte_end":128},{"index":1,"byte_start"