 and allow it to rejoin the international community. But there are some facts about Zarif that are less well-known. Here are six:. In September 2013, Zarif tweeted \\"Happy Rosh Hashanah,\\" referring to the Jewish New Year. That prompted Christine Pelosi, the daughter of House Minority Leader Nancy Pelosi, to respond with a tweet of her own: \\"Thanks. The New Year would be even sweeter if you would end Iran\'s Holocaust denial, sir.\\" And, perhaps to her surprise, Pelosi got a response. \\"Iran never denied it,\\" Zarif tweeted back. \\"The man who was perceived to be denying it is now gone. Happy New Year.\\" The reference was likely to former Iranian President Mahmoud Ahmadinejad, who had left office the previous month. Zarif was nominated to be foreign minister by Ahmadinejad\'s successor, Hassan Rouhami. His foreign ministry notes, perhaps defensively, that \\"due to the political and security conditions of the time, he decided to continue his education in the United States.\\" That is another way of saying that he was outside the country during the demonstrations against the Shah of Iran, which began in 1977, and during the Iranian Revolution, which drove the shah from power in 1979. Zarif left the country in 1977, received his undergraduate degree from San Francisco State University in 1981, his master\'s in international relations from the University of Denver in 1984 and his doctorate from the University of Denver in 1988. Both of his children were born in the United States. The website of the Iranian Foreign Ministry, which Zarif runs, cannot even agree with itself on when he was born. The first sentence of his official biography, perhaps in a nod to the powers that be in Tehran, says Zarif was \\"born to a religious traditional family in Tehran in 1959.\\" Later on the same page, however, his date of birth is listed as January 8, 1960. And the Iranian Diplomacy website says he was born in in 1961. So he is 54, 55 or maybe even 56. Whichever, he is still considerably younger than his opposite number, Kerry, who is 71. The feds investigated him over his alleged role in controlling the Alavi Foundation, a charitable organization. The U.S. Justice Department said the organization was secretly run on behalf of the Iranian government to launder money and get around U.S. sanctions. But last year, a settlement in the case, under which the foundation agreed to give a 36-story building in Manhattan along with other properties to the U.S. government, did not mention Zarif\'s name. Early in the Iranian Revolution, Zarif was among the students who took over the Iranian Consulate in San Francisco. The aim, says the website Iranian.com -- which cites Zarif\'s memoirs, titled \\"Mr. Ambassador\\" -- was to expel from the consulate people who were not sufficiently Islamic. Later, the website says, Zarif went to make a similar protest at the Iranian mission to the United Nations. In response, the Iranian ambassador to the United Nations offered him a job. In fact, he has now spent more time with Kerry than any other foreign minister in the world. And that amount of quality time will only increase as the two men, with help from other foreign ministers as well, try to meet a June 30 deadline for nailing down the details of the agreement they managed to outline this week in Switzerland.\\n","answer":"As the world watches the development of the Iran nuclear deal, the Iranian Foreign Minister Mohammad Javad Zarif has become a crucial figure. Zarif has been Secretary of State John Kerry\'s opposite number in negotiating a nuclear deal that could lead to an end to sanctions on Iran. Zarif has developed a reputation for being \'polished\' and \'jovial\', and has been hailed as the man who could bring Iran back into the international community. However, there are less well-known aspects to Zarif\'s career, including his tweet to mark the Jewish New Year in 2013, a move which triggered a discussion about Iran\'s Holocaust denial. Zarif was also nominated as Foreign Minister by the successor to the notorious former Iranian President Mahmoud Ahmadinejad. Zarif\'s own background includes a period of study in the US, where he earned an undergraduate degree from San Francisco State University and a doctorate from the University of Denver. Zarif is also reportedly under investigation by US authorities over his alleged role in controlling a charitable organization accused of money laundering.","source_segments":[{"index":0,"byte_start":0,"byte_end":114},{"index":1,"byte_start":114,"byte_end":162},{"index":2,"byte_start":162,"byte_end":386},{"index":3,"byte_start":386,"byte_end":468},{"index":4,"byte_start":468,"byte_end":551},{"index":5,"byte_start":551,"byte_end":664},{"index":6,"byte_start":664,"byte_end":752},{"index":7,"byte_start":752,"byte_end":868},{"index":8,"byte_start":868,"byte_end":931},{"index":9,"byte_start":931,"byte_end":946},{"index":10,"byte_start":946,"byte_end":1036},{"index":11,"byte_start":1036,"byte_end":1165},{"index":12,"byte_start":1165,"byte_end":1248},{"index":13,"byte_start":1248,"byte_end":1301},{"index":14,"byte_start":1301,"byte_end":1345},{"index":15,"byte_start":1345,"byte_end":1402},{"index":16,"byte_start":1402,"byte_end":1419},{"index":17,"byte_start":1419,"byte_end":1533},{"index":18,"byte_start":1533,"byte_end":1620},{"index":19,"byte_start":1620,"byte_end":1793},{"index":20,"byte_start":1793,"byte_end":2303},{"index":21,"byte_start":2303,"byte_end":2417},{"index":22,"byte_start":2417,"byte_end":2966},{"index":23,"byte_start":2966,"byte_end":3117},{"index":24,"byte_start":3117,"byte_end":3315},{"index":25,"byte_start":3315,"byte_end":3431},{"index":26,"byte_start":3431,"byte_end":3602},{"index":27,"byte_start":3602,"byte_end":3710},{"index":28,"byte_start":3710,"byte_end":3787},{"index":29,"byte_start":3787,"byte_end":3880},{"index":30,"byte_start":3880,"byte_end":4122}],"answer_sentence_indices":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6],"answer_offsets":[{"index":0,"byte_start":0,"byte_end":142}