secretly run on behalf of the Iranian government to launder money and get around U.S. sanctions. But last year, a settlement in the case, under which the foundation agreed to give a 36-story building in Manhattan along with other properties to the U.S. government, did not mention Zarif\'s name. Early in the Iranian Revolution, Zarif was among the students who took over the Iranian Consulate in San Francisco. The aim, says the website Iranian.com -- which cites Zarif\'s memoirs, titled \\"Mr. Ambassador\\" -- was to expel from the consulate people who were not sufficiently Islamic. Later, the website says, Zarif went to make a similar protest at the Iranian mission to the United Nations. In response, the Iranian ambassador to the United Nations offered him a job. In fact, he has now spent more time with Kerry than any other foreign minister in the world. And that amount of quality time will only increase as the two men, with help from other foreign ministers as well, try to meet a June 30 deadline for nailing down the details of the agreement they managed to outline this week in Switzerland.\\n","answer":"As the world watches the development of the Iran nuclear deal, the Iranian Foreign Minister Mohammad Javad Zarif has become a crucial figure. Zarif has been Secretary of State John Kerry\'s opposite number in negotiating a nuclear deal that could lead to an end to sanctions on Iran. Zarif has developed a reputation for being \'polished\' and \'jovial\', and has been hailed as the man who could bring Iran back into the international community. However, there are less well-known aspects to Zarif\'s career, including his tweet to mark the Jewish New Year in 2013, a move which triggered a discussion about Iran\'s Holocaust denial. Zarif was also nominated as Foreign Minister by the successor to the notorious former Iranian President Mahmoud Ahmadinejad. Zarif\'s own background includes a period of study in the US, where he earned an undergraduate degree from San Francis